 Diamonds and 4 of Clubs**. The board shows: **6♥, 6♠, A♣, 4♦**.  \nThis gives you the following possible hands:\n- A pair of **6s** on the board.\n- You have a **pair of 4s** (your 4♣ + 4♦ on board).\n- So your best hand is **Two Pair: Sixes and Fours**, with Jack kicker.\n\nHowever, your opponent raised by 312 on the turn (Round 2), which suggests strength. Given that the board has two 6s and now a paired low board, they might have:\n- A full house (if they hold a 6 or a pocket pair matching the board).\n- A strong overpair (like AA, but only one A on board).\n- Or possibly a bluff or semi-bluff with Ax.\n\nYour two pair is medium-strength at best. It’s vulnerable to:\n- Any A-x beating you via trips Aces,\n- Any 6-x making full house,\n- Even 4-x makes a better two pair (with higher kicker than Jack),\n- Or if opponent has 6-4, they already have a full house.\n\nYet, you checked through previous rounds, and opponent did not bet aggressively pre-flop or on flop — until now. Their raise on the turn suggests they are trying to build the pot or push you off a hand like middle pair or draw.\n\nYou have position (act after opponent in future actions), but this is likely the final decision point unless you raise and get re-raised.\n\nValid actions: Fold (-1), Check (0), Raise to 624 or more, or All-in (-2).\n\nCalling (checking here, since opponent\'s action was a raise and you\'re responding) would mean checking again, effectively calling 312 more chips? Wait — clarification needed.\n\nWait: Game state says "on Round 2, your opponent raised bet by 312". Round 2 is the Turn. Now it\'s your turn to act *after* that raise.\n\nSo current bet to call is **312**. To raise, you must make it at least **twice the previous raise**, so minimum raise is **624 total bet** (i.e., you must put in 624, meaning you add 312 more to match and then 312 more to raise). So raising requires putting in 624 total (a raise of 312 over their 312).\n\nBut your valid actions are listed as: [-2, -1, 0, 624, or any value above 624].\n\nNote: “0” here likely means **call the 312 bet** (since betting 0 wouldn’t make sense if there\'s an active bet). But in poker, when facing a bet, “0” usually isn\'t a valid action — unless it\'s interpreted as “call”.\n\nBut given the context, and that 0 was allowed in prior rounds when no bet was made, here “0” probably means **call the 312 bet**.\n\nSo options:\n- Fold: lose hand, lose 312 chips.\n- Call (0): pay 312 to see the river.\n- Raise to 624 or more: show strength, possibly force fold.\n- All-in: you have 19,900, so all-in is ~19,588 more (but represented as -2).\n\nNow evaluate:\n\nOption 1: Fold (-1)  \n→ Lose equity. Your hand (two pair) still has some showdown value, especially since few strong hands are likely. Folding here gives up too much value.\n\nOption 2: Call (0)  \n→ Pay 312 to see the river. Low cost. You’re getting correct pot odds if the pot is large enough. Let’s estimate pot size.\n\nWe don’t have exact pot size, but from history:\n- Pre-flop: both checked → pot small, maybe 200–400?\n- Flop: both checked → pot didn’t grow.\n- Turn: opponent bets 312 → so pot before that was likely around 400–600? After bet, pot becomes ~700–900. So calling 312 into ~900 pot = ~35% pot odds.\n\nWith two pair, you’re ahead of bluffs, Ax (without second pair), Kx, Qx, draws. But behind full houses, sets, and possibly trips Aces.\n\nStill, folding here would be too nitty given the price.\n\nOption 3: Raise to 624 or more  \n→ This would represent a strong hand — like you hit a set or have trips 4s or even a full house. But you only have two pair. Bluff-raising here is risky. Opponent showed strength by raising first. They are less likely to fold. Also, your hand has little improvement potential (only one 4 or one 6 left to improve to full house).\n\nRaising may lose you more chips when behind.\n\nOption 4: All-in (-2)  \n→ Overkill. Same issues as raise, but worse. Commits nearly all your stack with a vulnerable hand.\n\nBest move: **Call (0)** to see the river card. You’re getting reasonable odds, and your hand still has value. Folding loses too much equity; raising is too strong for your holding.\n\nReason:  \nCalling the 312 bet costs relatively little compared to stack size and offers a chance to see the river and potentially win with two pair or improve to a full house if a 4 or 6 hits. Raising lacks justification given hand strength and opponent initiative. Folding surrenders too much equity for the price.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
