s allowed (but opponent might have bet).\nWait — your valid actions include **-2, -1, 0, 100, or higher raises**. The presence of "0" and raise options suggests **no pending bet** — meaning you can check or bet. So the opponent did **not** bet on the river — you’re first to act.\n\nThat implies: After you called the 312 raise on the turn, the river card was dealt, and now it’s your turn with no bet yet — so you can **check** or **bet**.\n\nBoard: 6♥, 6♠, A♣, 4♦, 8♠ — all five cards are present. So yes, river complete.\n\nYour hand: J♦, 4♣ → makes:  \n- Two pair: 6s and 4s, with J kicker.\n\nPossible better hands:\n- Any A-x: two pair (A+6) > your two pair (6+4)\n- 8-x: two pair (8+6) > your (6+4)\n- 6-8, 6-6, 8-8, A-A, 4-4, etc. → full houses\n- 5-7 → straight (5-6-7-8-9? No 7 or 5 on board) — no straight possible\n- Flush? Only two of any suit — max two diamonds, two clubs, two hearts, two spades — no flush possible\n\nSo no flush or straight on board. Best possible hands are full houses or two pair.\n\nBut crucially: **you have two pair, but it\'s medium-weak**. And you’ve shown passivity throughout.\n\nOpponent raised on the turn — very suspicious. Likely has:\n- A-6 (full house),\n- 6-8 (full house),\n- A-A, 8-8, or 4-4 for trips,\n- Or A-x for top two pair.\n\nIt’s unlikely they’re bluffing given the line.\n\nYour best move options:\n1. **Check (0)**: Safest. Let opponent bet if strong. You can fold or call depending on bet size. But if you check and they bet big, you’ll likely have to fold anyway.\n2. **Small bet (e.g., 100)**: Could be a blocking bet, but unusual for your passive line. Might get called by worse (like a single pair), but more likely to get raised by better hands.\n3. **Fold (-1)**: Too early — you should at least see if opponent will check behind.\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Overkill. You have no fold equity likely — better hands won’t fold, worse hands won’t call.\n5. **Larger raise**: Doesn’t make sense — you’re not representing strength.\n\nBest play: **Check (0)** to see what opponent does. Since you\'re out of position and have a marginal hand, checking keeps the pot manageable and lets you react.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a weak two pair that likely loses to stronger two pair or full houses. The opponent showed strength by raising on the turn. Checking on the river allows you to control losses and potentially see a showdown if they check back. Betting risks getting raised off the hand or only getting calls from better hands.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
