nk they have showdown value.\n\nBut given the board, many draws completed or failed clearly.\n\nBest play: **Small bet for value** — aim to extract chips from hands like:\n- A-K (top pair, good kicker)\n- A-Q\n- Q-J\n- K-Q\n- Bluffing missed spade draws\n\nEven though you're behind full houses and straights, those are relatively narrow ranges.\n\nOpponent likely doesn’t have trips already on flop — you raised early, so unpaired hands may have folded.\n\nBut they called every street — consistent with drawing or slow-playing.\n\nStill, **checking loses value** against weaker pairs — they might just check back.\n\nSo **betting 100** (minimum allowed raise) is a good balance:  \n- Small enough not to overcommit  \n- Large enough to get action from marginal hands  \n- May fold out pure bluffs\n\nGoing all-in (-2) is too much — you have 19,250 chips left — going all-in here would be massive overbet and likely only called by better hands.\n\nFolding (-1) is nonsense — you have three of a kind.\n\nChecking (0) risks free showdown — missed draws won’t pay you off.\n\nThus, **bet 100** is optimal.\n\nReason: You have a strong hand (three of a kind) that is ahead of most one-pair or two-pair hands, but vulnerable to full houses and straights. A small bet of 100 maximizes value from worse hands while minimizing risk against better ones. Opponent has shown passivity, so a small probe bet is likely to get calls from weaker holdings.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
